    <!-- ═══ WHAT IS COMPRESSION ═══ -->
    <section>
      <h2 id="what-is-compression">What Is Golf Ball Compression and Why It Matters</h2>
      <p>Golf ball compression is a number (typically 30-110) that measures how much the ball deforms at impact. A low-compression ball (35-65) squishes more easily, which means slower swing speeds can fully compress it and transfer maximum energy. A high-compression ball (85-102) resists deformation, which means faster swingers get more energy return — but slower swingers literally cannot compress it enough to get the same benefit.</p>
      <p>Think of it like a trampoline: a soft trampoline (low compression) gives a big bounce even with a gentle jump. A stiff competition trampoline (high compression) gives a bigger bounce — but only if you jump hard enough to flex it. Jump gently on a stiff trampoline and you barely bounce. That is exactly what happens when a 75 mph swinger plays a 100-compression ball.</p>
      <p>Compression is measured by applying a specific load to the ball and measuring deformation in millimeters. There is no universal standard — Titleist, Callaway, and Bridgestone each use slightly different methods. The numbers on our chart are normalized to be comparable across brands, based on independent testing data and published specifications.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="find-swing-speed">How to Find Your Swing Speed</h2>
      <h3>Measure With a Launch Monitor</h3>
      <p>The most accurate method. Budget options like the <a href="/best-launch-monitors-no-subscription/">Shot Scope LM1 ($199, no subscription)</a> display your club speed on a built-in screen. Most golf stores also offer free swing speed checks — ask at your local Golf Galaxy or PGA Tour Superstore.</p>
      <h3>Estimate from Your 7-Iron Carry Distance</h3>
      <p>If you don't have a launch monitor, your 7-iron carry distance is a reliable proxy. Use the "I don't know my speed" button in the calculator above, or reference this table:</p>
